WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.080
One agent handles one job well.

2
00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:04.179
But some jobs are actually several independent jobs at once — that's what swarms are for.

3
00:00:04.179 --> 00:00:07.000
Let's see when that's worth it, and when it's just an expensive way to go slower.

4
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:16.000
Turn on Swarm and, instead of one agent working through a job step by step, several agents run at the same time, each on their own slice of it.

5
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:22.277
The distinction that decides whether it helps: genuinely parallel work, like reviewing twelve independent files at once, is exactly what a swarm speeds up.

6
00:00:22.277 --> 00:00:29.000
Sequential work, where step two needs step one's answer, gets no benefit — a swarm there is just slower and more expensive than one agent working through it in order.

7
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:35.185
While a swarm runs, a shared view shows exactly which file each agent is touching right now, so nothing gets edited twice by accident.

8
00:00:35.185 --> 00:00:41.000
And if one agent changes a file another depends on, that second agent is explicitly told about the change before it continues.

9
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.723
The gotcha, and it's a cost one: several agents means several conversations billed at the same time, not one.

10
00:00:45.723 --> 00:00:47.890
A twelve-file swarm can be twelve concurrent runs.

11
00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:54.000
That's fine when the job is genuinely parallel and time matters — but it is not a default choice, and it's not free just because it's faster.

12
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:55.065
Agents and swarms handle work.

13
00:00:55.065 --> 00:00:59.290
But some things aren't work at all — they're reference material the model should only load when it's actually relevant.

14
00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:00.000
That's skills, next.

